===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|en|enm|savouren}}, {{m|enm|savour|t=to be tasty; to give flavour; to have flavour; to taste or enjoy the taste of (something); to be able to smell; to have a smell; to smell (something); to enjoy; to give pleasure; to know, understand; to learn; to have a spiritual experience}},<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=sāvǒuren|pos=v|id=MED38656}}</ref> from {{der|en|xno|savorer}}, {{m|xno|savourer}}, {{der|en|frm|savorer}}, {{m|frm|savourer}}, and {{der|en|fro|savorer}}, {{m|fro|savourer|t=to give flavour; to have flavour; to be pleasant; to taste or enjoy the taste of (something); to enjoy; to know, perceive}} (modern {{cog|fr|savourer}}), from {{der|en|LL.|sapōrāre}}, the {{glossary|present}} {{glossary|active}} {{glossary|infinitive}} of {{m|LL.|sapōrō|t=to give flavour, make tasty; to give pleasure}}, from {{der|en|la|sapor|t=flavour; taste; scent; smell}} (see [[#Etymology 1|etymology 1]])<ref>{{R:OED Online|entry=savour {{!}} savor|pos=v|id=3626738198|date=December 2023|nodot=1}}; {{R:Lexico|pos=v}}</ref> + {{m|la|-ō|pos=suffix forming regular first-{{glossary|conjugation}} {{glossary|verb}}s}}.

